<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallienus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 218–268</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Publius Licinius Egnatius Gallienus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gallienus was Roman emperor from 253 to 268, first with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> and alone after Valerian's capture by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in 260. His reign saw the worst of the 3rd-century crisis: the Gallic and Palmyrene regions broke away, and Alemanni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and others invaded. He defeated the Alemanni near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, reorganised the cavalry, and was murdered by his officers in 268.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallienus.jpg" alt="Gallienus" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_gallieno_dalla_casa_delle_vestali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gallienus was born into a wealthy and traditional senatorial family, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> and Mariniana. His exact birth date is unknown, but two late sources give his age at death as about 50, which puts it around 218. About ten years before his accession he married Cornelia Salonina, and they had three sons. When Valerian was proclaimed emperor in September 253, he had the Senate raise Gallienus to the rank of Augustus. Father and son divided the empire, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a> had done a century earlier: Valerian went east to meet the Persian threat, and Gallienus stayed in the west to repel Germanic tribes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Between 258 and 260 Gallienus defeated the usurper Ingenuus in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, but his absence left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> open. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> broke into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, some reaching southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the Alemanni crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in the first invasion of the Italian peninsula since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, 500 years before. An improvised army raised by the Senate repelled them from the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and Gallienus's army defeated them at Mediolanum. They did not trouble the empire again for ten years.</p><p>In 260 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> was defeated and captured at Edessa by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, and control of the whole empire passed to Gallienus. He defeated the eastern usurpers Macrianus Major and Lucius Mussius Aemilianus in 261 and 262, but the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a>, which stayed independent until 274, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> became independent in nearly every respect. In 267 to 269 the Heruli and others invaded by sea and ravaged Greek cities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 268 the cavalry commander Aureolus proclaimed himself emperor at Mediolanum, was defeated and besieged in the city, and during the siege Gallienus was stabbed to death by the officer Cecropius. The sources agree that most of his officials wanted him dead. Ancient historians treated him badly, and the Historia Augusta, largely fictional, makes him a lover of luxury; this hostility was partly due to the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>.</p><p>He was the first to commission primarily cavalry units, the Comitatenses, which could be sent anywhere in the empire quickly, and Aurelius Victor reports that he forbade senators to become military commanders. He also issued the first official declaration of tolerance of Christians, restoring their places of worship and cemeteries. Some historians now see him more favourably.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gallienus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 260 a great raid reached Italy before <b>Gallienus</b> defeated it near Milan, and in 268 Claudius II defeated another army near Lake Garda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Marcomannic Wars some Marcomanni were settled in Italy and other parts of the empire, <b>Gallienus</b> settled others in Pannonia Superior, and by the late 4th century at least some had become Christians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Votive coins scattered over the floor of the mithraeum at Pons Sarravi (Sarrebourg) in Gallia Belgica run from <b>Gallienus</b> to Theodosius I: when the temple was destroyed, Christians apparently regarded the coins as polluted and left them behind.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His pontificate saw the toleration issued by <b>Gallienus</b> in 260, and he assigned presbyters to individual parishes.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-22">22 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He besieged Cologne and killed <b>Gallienus</b>' son Saloninus there and won the loyalty of Gaul, Germania, Britain and Hispania.</blockquote><a href="/state/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Persian king Shapur I captured the emperor Valerian in 260, the Palmyrene noble Odaenathus defeated the retreating Persians and crushed the usurpers in the East, earning from <b>Gallienus</b> the title corrector of the whole East.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Mediolanum, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.46,9.19&amp;year=250" aria-label="Mediolanum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gallienus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.46,9.19&amp;year=250">What was here in 250 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
